hypotheses.statement:
One can fix $\alpha_0$ sufficiently large and then $\ell_*>0$ sufficiently small so that every normalized nonnegative bosonic ground function satisfies, for every $A\subset\mathcal L$, $$\label{set:low-count-bound}
 \mu\{n_{B_v}<\ell_*D\text{ for every }v\in A\}
           \le (C/D^2)^{|A|}.$$

The count estimates of Proposition <a href="#en:energy-outputs" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="en:energy-outputs">13</a> imply $$\begin{aligned}
 \frac1{|\mathcal C_R|}\sum_{v\in\mathcal C_R}
       \mathbb E|T_v^{\mathrm{rel}}-1|^2
 &\le C\bigl(D^{-1-w/6}+D^{-1+x}R^{-3}\bigr),
       \label{palm:R-count-moment}\\
 \frac1{|\mathcal C_d|}\sum_{v\in\mathcal C_d}
       \mathbb E|T_v^{\mathrm{rel}}-1|^4
 &\le C_j\bigl(D^{-1-u/6}+D^{-2+2x}\bigr).
       \label{palm:d-count-moment}
\end{aligned}$$ For each fixed integer $q$, products of at most $q$ relative counts can be bounded by $C_jD^{qx}$ at an error smaller than every prescribed negative power of $D$.

For some $c_3>0$, the mass of this common measure satisfies, after averaging over $(v,w)\in\mathcal A_{K,R}$, $$\label{long:mass-bound}
 \nu_{vw}(1)\ge
       \mathbb E[T_v^{\rm rel}T_w^{\rm rel}]-C D^{-1-c_3}-o_K(1).$$

For some $c_4>0$, the left augmented likelihood satisfies $$\label{long:second-moment}
 \int\ell_{v,w}^2\,\mathrm dP_{{\rm aug},v}
       \le \mathbb E[T_v^{\rm rel}(T_w^{\rm rel})^2]
                           +CD^{-1-c_4}+o_K(1)$$ after averaging over $\mathcal A_{K,R}$.

outputs.exact_statement:
For every $a>0$, every bounded continuous real function $f$ on $\mathbb R^3$, and every $\varepsilon>0$, there is $\rho_0(a,f,\varepsilon)>0$ with the following property. Fix $0<\rho<\rho_0$ and put $\eta=\rho a^3$. For every sequence $N_j,L_j\longrightarrow\infty$ with $N_j/L_j^3\longrightarrow\rho$, $$\label{intro:main-limit}
 \limsup_{j\to\infty}\ \sup_{\Gamma\in\mathcal G_{N_j,L_j,a}}
 \left|
 \frac{1}{N_j\sqrt\eta}
 \sum_{\substack{k\in(2\pi/L_j)\mathbb Z^3\\k\ne0}}
 f\!\left(\frac{k}{\sqrt{8\pi\rho a}}\right)n_{\Gamma,L_j}(k)
       -\int_{\mathbb R^3}f\,\mathrm d\nu_{\mathrm{Bog}}
 \right|\le\varepsilon.$$

One can fix $\alpha_0$ sufficiently large and then $\ell_*>0$ sufficiently small so that every normalized nonnegative bosonic ground function satisfies, for every $A\subset\mathcal L$, $$\label{set:low-count-bound}
 \mu\{n_{B_v}<\ell_*D\text{ for every }v\in A\}
           \le (C/D^2)^{|A|}.$$ The estimate holds for all sufficiently large $D$, uniformly in $K$ and the ground function.

For each particle label $i$ let $d_i(X)=\min_{k\ne i}d_K(x_i,x_k)$, with the value $+\infty$ if $N=1$. Given $\theta>0$, one can choose $b_*>0$ sufficiently small, and then $D$ sufficiently large, so that the set $$\mathcal Q(X)=\left\{v\in\mathcal L:
    \#\{i:d_K(x_i,v)\le5,\ d_i(X)\le b_*D^{-1/3}\}
          \ge\theta D\right\}$$ satisfies, for every $A\subset\mathcal L$, $$\label{set:close-tail-bound}
 \mu\{A\subset\mathcal Q\}\le p_D^{|A|},
 \qquad
 p_D=\bigl(C_\theta b_*^{-2}D^{-4/3}\bigr)^{1/q_*}\longrightarrow0,$$ where $q_*$ is an absolute positive integer. The uniformity is the same as in Proposition <a href="#set:count-mgf" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="set:count-mgf">6</a>.

There are arbitrarily small fixed $u>0$ and then $0<w<u/10000$ for which the following statements hold. Use the nested partitions fixed in <a href="#set:nested-lengths" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="set:nested-lengths">[set:nested-lengths]</a>, with $B\asymp D^u$, $R\asymp D^w$, and $d=1/j$. For every normalized nonnegative bosonic ground vector, $$\begin{aligned}
 \frac1{|\mathcal C_R|}\sum_{A\in\mathcal C_R}
       \mathbb E(q_A-1)^2&\le C D^{-1-w/6},\label{en:R-count}\\
 \frac1{|\mathcal C_d|}\sum_{A\in\mathcal C_d}
       \mathbb E(q_A-1)^4&\le C_jD^{-1-u/6}.\label{en:d-count}
\end{aligned}$$ For every normalized, possibly complex, bosonic ground vector, $$\begin{aligned}
 \frac1V\left\langle\sum_{i=1}^N W_{R,i}\right\rangle
       &=\frac{8}{3\sqrt\pi}\alpha^{3/2}+o(1),\label{en:R-occupation}\\
 \frac1V\left\langle\sum_{i=1}^N W_{d,i}\right\rangle
       &\le \epsilon_j+o_j(1),\qquad \epsilon_j\longrightarrow0
                \quad(j\longrightarrow\infty).\label{en:d-occupation}
\end{aligned}$$ Here the thermodynamic limit is taken first, at fixed dilution; the remainders then tend to zero as $D\to\infty$, and $o_j(1)$ has this meaning with $j$ fixed. The bounds are uniform over the indicated ground vectors and along every density-convergent thermodynamic sequence. A sufficiently large volume threshold may depend on the dilution and on $j$.

With the exponents and partitions of Proposition <a href="#en:energy-outputs" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="en:energy-outputs">13</a>, every normalized nonnegative bosonic ground function satisfies $$\label{palm:local-rms-comparison}
 \frac1{|\mathcal C_d|}\sum_{v\sim w}
       \int\frac{|s_v-s_w|^4}{s_v^2+s_w^2}\,\mathrm dY'
                 \le C_jD^{-1-u/8}.$$ Here $v\sim w$ ranges over ordered adjacent $d$-cells, and the quotient is zero when both functions vanish. The sum may be restricted to pairs in the same $R$-cell. The bound is uniform in the ground function and volume, with the limiting convention of Section <a href="#set:section" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="set:section">2</a>.

The count estimates of Proposition <a href="#en:energy-outputs" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="en:energy-outputs">13</a> imply $$\begin{aligned}
 \frac1{|\mathcal C_R|}\sum_{v\in\mathcal C_R}
       \mathbb E|T_v^{\mathrm{rel}}-1|^2
 &\le C\bigl(D^{-1-w/6}+D^{-1+x}R^{-3}\bigr),
       \label{palm:R-count-moment}\\
 \frac1{|\mathcal C_d|}\sum_{v\in\mathcal C_d}
       \mathbb E|T_v^{\mathrm{rel}}-1|^4
 &\le C_j\bigl(D^{-1-u/6}+D^{-2+2x}\bigr).
       \label{palm:d-count-moment}
\end{aligned}$$ For each fixed integer $q$, products of at most $q$ relative counts can be bounded by $C_jD^{qx}$ at an error smaller than every prescribed negative power of $D$. This assertion holds in any of the averaged estimates involving a fixed number of cells used below.

The left-output likelihood, with the weight $h_{w,j}$ included but the divisor $T_w$ omitted, is, on admissible reference values, $$\label{palm:likelihood-formula}
 L_S(z;\xi)
  =\int a_S(o;\xi)h_{w,j}(o)
          k_S(o;\xi,z)\prod_{l\in S}B_l(\mathrm do_l).$$ It satisfies $$\label{palm:centered-identity}
 F_S=L_S-h_{w,j},\qquad
       \mathbb E_\lambda[F_S\mid\omega_{S^c}]=0.$$ These assertions hold after removing any specified collection of outside checks, provided the same removals are used in the reference, the original-path integral, and the insertion kernels. Moreover, $$\label{palm:local-likelihood-sup}
                    0\le L_S\le C_xD^{C\sqrt x}.$$

For adjacent $d$-cells $v,w$, let an overbar denote the average over the route and interior label choices at fixed tags and datum. Restrict the expectation to $\mathcal G_{v,w}$ and retained first tags. Equivalently, set the complete centered summand to zero outside this event. Then $$\label{palm:fourth-centered}
 \mathbb E\left[\mathbf 1_{\mathcal G_{v,w}}
       \sum_{i\in\mathcal I}\frac{h_{v,i}}{T_v}
    \left|\sum_{j\ne i}\frac{\overline{F_S}}{T_w}\right|^4\right]
          \le C_{x,j}D^{-1-1/20},$$ provided $x$ is sufficiently small. The candidate second tags are the labels in $\mathcal I$ whose mean-sites lie in the required enlargement of $w$.

For sufficiently small $x>0$, depending on the previously chosen exponent $u$, the measures above satisfy $$\label{palm:augmented-fourth}
 \frac1{|\mathcal C_d|}\sum_{v\sim w}
     \left\{\int|\ell_v-1|^4\,\mathrm dP_{\mathrm{aug},v}
          +\int|\ell_w-1|^4\,\mathrm dP_{\mathrm{aug},w}\right\}
       \le C_jD^{-1-u/8}.$$ The summation can be restricted to adjacent cells in the same $R$-cell. The bound is uniform in volume and in the nonnegative ground function, with the limiting convention of Section <a href="#set:section" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="set:section">2</a>.

There are $p_0,\kappa,c,C>0$ and $R_0$ with the following property. Suppose $R\ge R_0$, $K\ge C_0R$, and <a href="#rt:bad-law" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="rt:bad-law">[rt:bad-law]</a> holds with $p\le p_0$. Put $h=\lfloor R^{1/2}\rfloor$. There is an event $\mathcal E$, determined by $\mathcal D,z,Q$, on which the construction below gives simple good nearest-neighbor routes $\pi_y$ from $z$ to every $y\in Q$. Conditional on the datum, routes are sampled using fresh independent random variables. The event includes the requirement that the $10R$-neighborhoods of $z$ and $Q$ contain no bad sites.*



*For a route $\pi=(v_0,\ldots,v_m)$ starting at $z$, set $$a(\pi)=\max\{i:d_\infty(v_i,z)\le h\},\qquad
 \pi^{\rm ch}=\{v_i:i>a(\pi)\}.$$ Its silent portion $(v_0,\ldots,v_{a(\pi)})$ has at most $Ch$ vertices. The analogous silent portion read backwards from $y$ also has at most $Ch$ vertices, and the two portions are disjoint. Let $\pi^{\rm ch,q}$ consist of vertices whose path index is within $q$ of an index in $\pi^{\rm ch}$. For independently sampled routes $\pi_y,\pi_{y'}$, define $$\begin{aligned}
 J&=\sum_{v\in\pi_y}\sum_{v'\in\pi_{y'}}
       \mathbf1_{\{d_\infty(v,v')\le r_c\}},\\
 J_*&=\sum_{v\in\pi_y^{\rm ch,q}}
       \sum_{v'\in\pi_{y'}^{\rm ch,q}}
       \mathbf1_{\{d_\infty(v,v')\le r_c\}}.
\end{aligned}$$ For every fixed pair $y,y'\in Q$, $$\label{rt:exponential}
 \mathbb E_{\mathcal D}\!\left[
  \mathbf1_{\mathcal E}\,
  \mathbb E_{\rm routes}(e^{\kappa J}\mid\mathcal D)\right]\le C.$$ For every deterministic probability mass function $\omega$ on $Q\times Q$ satisfying $\omega(y,y')\le C_\omega R^{-6}$, $$\label{rt:rare}
 \sum_{y,y'\in Q}\omega(y,y')\,
 \mathbb E_{\mathcal D}\!\left[
  \mathbf1_{\mathcal E}\,
  \mathbb P_{\rm routes}(J_*>0\mid\mathcal D)\right]
 \le CC_\omega R^{-c_g},\qquad
 c_g=\frac12\left(\frac{\log2}{\log3}-\frac12\right)>0.$$ The same conclusions hold with a common terminal endpoint, reading the routes in reverse order.*


*If $\mathcal M$ denotes just the stated bad-free margin event, then $$\label{rt:rejection}
 \mathbb P(\mathcal M\setminus\mathcal E)
 \le K^3(Ap)^{\lfloor K/(10(r_c+2))\rfloor}
       +CR^3e^{-cR}.$$ Here $A$ is fixed and $p_0$ is chosen so that $Ap_0<1$.

Put $\sigma=\log2/\log3$. For every integer $T\ge1$ there is an integer-valued process $(S_j)_{j=0}^T$, with $S_0=0$ and $|S_{j+1}-S_j|=1$, such that $$\label{rt:prediction-bound}
 \sup_{x\in\mathbb Z}
 \mathbb P(S_{u+k}=x\mid S_0,\ldots,S_u)
 \le 6^\sigma k^{-\sigma}
 \quad(k\ge1,\ u+k\le T).$$

At each time $s\in\{0,\ldots,T\}$, let $A_s(l)$ be a union of at most two deterministic squares of maximum radius $l$, with arbitrary centers, in the transverse torus. There is a constant $C_1$, independent of their centers and of $T\le K/2$, such that $$\label{rt:ordered-bound}
 \sum_{0\le s_1<\cdots<s_j\le T}
 \mathbb P\{X_{s_i}\in A_{s_i}(l_i),\ 1\le i\le j\}
 \le \prod_{i=1}^jC_1(1+l_i)^2.$$ In particular, for $a_l\ge0$, if $C_1\sum_{l\ge0}a_l(1+l)^2<1$, then $$\label{rt:ordered-product}
 \mathbb E\prod_{s=0}^T
 \left(1+\sum_{l\ge0}a_l\mathbf1_{\{X_s\in A_s(l)\}}\right)
 \le \left(1-C_1\sum_{l\ge0}a_l(1+l)^2\right)^{-1}.$$

For sufficiently small $x>0$ relative to the previously fixed exponents, the functions $s_v$ of Lemma <a href="#palm:reference" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="palm:reference">24</a> satisfy $$\label{long:hellinger}
 \frac1{|\mathcal A_{K,R}|}\sum_{(v,w)\in\mathcal A_{K,R}}
       \int |s_v(Y')-s_w(Y')|^2\,\mathrm dY'
       =o(D^{-1})+o_K(1).$$ The $o(D^{-1})$ is uniform over the nonnegative ground vectors and over the fixed window of $\alpha$. The thermodynamic error is taken to zero first.

For some $c_3>0$, the mass of this common measure satisfies, after averaging over $(v,w)\in\mathcal A_{K,R}$, $$\label{long:mass-bound}
 \nu_{vw}(1)\ge
       \mathbb E[T_v^{\rm rel}T_w^{\rm rel}]-C D^{-1-c_3}-o_K(1).$$

If $S\cap T=\varnothing$, then $$\label{long:disjoint-normalizers}
       1\le\frac{Z_S^-Z_T^-}{Z_U}
                    \le \exp(CB_{ST}).$$ If $q=|S\cap T|$ is arbitrary, then $$\label{long:shared-normalizers}
       \frac{Z_S^-Z_T^-}{Z_U}
                    \le C^q\exp(CB_{ST}).$$ All normalizers are strictly positive. In the disjoint case, if $H$ is any measurable function of the latent originals with $0\le H\le1$, and $A^- = A_S^- A_T^-$, then $$\label{long:tag-integral-difference}
 0\le \frac1{Z_U}\int H(A^- - A_U)\,\mathrm dB_U
                       \le C e^{CB_{ST}} B_{ST}.$$

Use the same latent originals and the same generated variables in the original and separate protocols. The original integrand is bounded by the separate integrand times a nonnegative factor $\mathcal R$ satisfying $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathcal R&\le e^{C D^{-19/100}J},
                                  \label{long:factor-exponential}\\
 \mathcal R&\le 1+C e^{C D^{-19/100}J}(B_{ST}+M_{ST}).
                                  \label{long:factor-increment}
\end{aligned}$$ The assertion is for the product of the two protocols as well. A transition with no removed check has ratio exactly one.

Let $\mu(\mathrm dx)$ be a probability measure of latent variables, followed by a finite ordered list of subprobability kernels. At stage $r$ the kernel generates a primary variable $z_r$ and arbitrary auxiliary variables. Let $Q$ be a set of stages. Suppose that, for every latent input and every allowed preceding history, the primary marginal at $r\in Q$ is at most $A_r
u_r(x,\mathrm dz_r)$, where $
u_r$ is a subprobability kernel depending on $x$ but not on the preceding history. Then the joint measure of the selected primary outputs is at most $$\label{long:reverse-kernel}
       \left(\prod_{r\in Q}A_right)
                   \int\mu(\mathrm dx)\prod_{r\in Q}
u_r(x,\mathrm dz_r).$$

Divide the separate output measure $M_S^-$ by its reference normalizer $Z_S^-$, and omit its original tag indicator if desired. For any $q$ specified primary output labels, its marginal is dominated by $$\label{long:marginal-bound}
                 G_D^q\bigotimes_{l\text{ specified}} B_l,
              \qquad G_D=C D^{C\sqrt x}.$$ The same assertion holds for $T$, and the constants are independent of the number of unselected outputs.

At almost every fixed admissible outside of $U=S\cup T$, the likelihoods of the two original transports satisfy the following bounds. If $S,T$ are disjoint, then $$\label{long:disjoint-product}
 \mathbb E[L_SL_T\mid U^c]\le
 \mathbb E[h_{w,j}h_{w,j'}\mid U^c]
 +C e^{C D^{-19/100}J}
          \bigl(B_{ST}+J_*b_D(1+t^{-3/2})\bigr).$$ If $q=|S\cap T|\ge1$, then $$\label{long:shared-product}
          \mathbb E[L_SL_T\mid U^c]
                     \le e^{C D^{-19/100}J}G_D^q,$$ after increasing the fixed constant in $G_D$.

For some $c_4>0$, the left augmented likelihood satisfies $$\label{long:second-moment}
 \int\ell_{v,w}^2\,\mathrm dP_{{\rm aug},v}
       \le \mathbb E[T_v^{\rm rel}(T_w^{\rm rel})^2]
                           +CD^{-1-c_4}+o_K(1)$$ after averaging over $\mathcal A_{K,R}$. Its mass satisfies <a href="#long:mass-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="long:mass-bound">[long:mass-bound]</a>, and the same estimates hold with $v,w$ exchanged.

Let $\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space and let $f_z\in\mathcal H$, $z\in(\mathbb Z/M\mathbb Z)^3$. Set $$I_M=\{h\in\{0,\ldots,M-1\}:.30M\le h\le .45M\},
 \qquad \overline f=M^{-3}\sum_z f_z.$$ For all sufficiently large $M$, there is an absolute constant $C$ such that $$\label{asm:far-variance}
 \frac1{M^3}\sum_z\|f_z-\overline f\|_{\mathcal H}^2
 \le \frac{C}{M^3|I_M|M^2}
      \sum_z\sum_{h_1\in I_M}\sum_{h_2,h_3\in\mathbb Z/M\mathbb Z}
             \|f_{z+h}-f_z\|_{\mathcal H}^2.$$

For any finite nonempty array of nonnegative numbers $y_i$, with uniform averaging denoted by $\mathbb E_i$, put $a=\mathbb E_i y_i^2$ and $m=\mathbb E_i y_i$. Then $$\label{asm:rms-inequality}
 (\sqrt a-m)^2\le\frac12\mathbb E_{i,i'}
           \frac{(y_i-y_{i'})^4}{y_i^2+y_{i'}^2}.$$ The quotient is defined to be zero when its denominator is zero. Furthermore, for every $y,z\ge0$, $$\label{asm:sqrt-comparison}
 |\sqrt y-\sqrt z|^4
 \le\frac{(y-z)^4}{y^2+z^2}
 \le8|\sqrt y-\sqrt z|^4.$$

If $K>2(B+H)$ and $|h|\le H$, then $$\label{mom:shift-norm}
 \left\|T_h-\operatorname{Avg}_{\mathcal C\in\mathcal S}
                        T_h^{\mathcal C}\right\|
          \le C\frac{H+R}{B}.$$
